WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.438
Welcome to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:10.056
This is Chapter One, the history of Denmark, Episode One: from the Vikings to the absolute monarchy.

3
00:00:10.606 --> 00:00:15.568
Let's begin with a remarkable fact, because it frames everything that follows.

4
00:00:15.518 --> 00:00:22.381
The Danish state has existed as an independent state, without interruption, for more than one thousand years.

5
00:00:22.381 --> 00:00:26.293
Seen from an international perspective, that is quite unique.

6
00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:30.931
Over that long story, Denmark has had several different forms of government.

7
00:00:30.931 --> 00:00:34.843
But every single one of them had the monarchy as its form of state.

8
00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:37.393
What changed was the role the king played.

9
00:00:37.393 --> 00:00:44.394
Today, the king is still formally the head of state, but it is the elected politicians who hold the political power.

10
00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:51.231
Denmark has also spent most of its history as a power in the Nordic region and around the Baltic Sea.

11
00:00:51.181 --> 00:00:58.632
It once held many different population groups, and has gradually been reduced to a small state with a fairly uniform population.

12
00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:04.764
Hold on to that arc, from regional power to small state, because we are going to watch it unfold.

13
00:01:05.314 --> 00:01:08.089
One small tip about the textbook itself.

14
00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:13.177
When a year range appears in brackets after a king or queen, it means their reign.

15
00:01:13.177 --> 00:01:20.952
So Frederik the Third, with the years sixteen forty-eight to seventy, reigned from sixteen forty-eight to sixteen seventy.

16
00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:24.852
For anyone else, the years in brackets are their lifetime.

17
00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:33.846
For example, the author Karen Blixen, eighteen eighty-five to nineteen sixty-two, lived from 1885 to 1962.

18
00:01:34.396 --> 00:01:40.884
Now, our story starts in the Viking Age, roughly the year 750 to the year 1050.

19
00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:46.896
This is the period when Denmark was gathered under one king, and power began to be centralised.

20
00:01:46.896 --> 00:01:52.234
The Vikings sailed out across Europe, where they traded, raided, and settled.

21
00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:56.088
And it was also in this period that most Danes became Christian.

22
00:01:56.638 --> 00:01:59.788
First, a correction to the popular image.

23
00:01:59.738 --> 00:02:04.888
Vikings are often described as brutal warriors, and some of them certainly were.

24
00:02:04.888 --> 00:02:15.018
But in reality, the vast majority of people in the Viking Age were farmers who worked the land and kept animals, or they were merchants who bought and sold goods.

25
00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.718
So how did a kingdom take shape?

26
00:02:17.668 --> 00:02:20.406
The first known king was named Angantyr.

27
00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:26.531
Already around the year 700, he gathered part of the area that would later become Denmark.

28
00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:30.843
The word "Denmark" itself is known from the end of the 800s.

29
00:02:30.843 --> 00:02:37.256
"Dan" comes from "daner", the people who lived in the Danish area, and "mark" means border area.

30
00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:40.356
So Denmark means "the Danes' borderland".

31
00:02:40.948 --> 00:02:49.011
The first time the name Denmark appears inside the country's own borders is on a so-called runestone from around the year 955.

32
00:02:48.961 --> 00:02:53.398
It was raised by King Gorm den Gamle, in memory of his wife, Thyra.

33
00:02:53.398 --> 00:02:58.986
A runestone is called that because the text is written in runes, the writing signs of the Viking Age.

34
00:02:58.986 --> 00:03:01.911
Gorm and Thyra's son was Harald Blåtand.

35
00:03:01.911 --> 00:03:08.348
He is regarded as the first king to rule all of Denmark, and he was also king over parts of Norway.

36
00:03:08.348 --> 00:03:14.536
Around the year 965, in the 900s, Harald raised a runestone of his own.

37
00:03:14.536 --> 00:03:18.240
Both stones stand today in the town of Jelling, in Jutland.

38
00:03:18.796 --> 00:03:26.321
To protect his kingdom against enemies, Harald Blåtand built a series of ring fortresses around the year 980.

39
00:03:26.271 --> 00:03:27.833
Remember the names.

40
00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:30.708
Aggersborg and Fyrkat in North Jutland.

41
00:03:30.708 --> 00:03:32.496
Nonnebakken on Funen.

42
00:03:32.496 --> 00:03:35.233
And Trelleborg and Borgring on Zealand.

43
00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:38.659
Trelleborg lies near Slagelse in West Zealand.

44
00:03:38.659 --> 00:03:42.996
By the standards of the time, these were huge building projects.

45
00:03:42.996 --> 00:03:49.921
And they tell us something important: the king had the power to collect taxes and to organise huge amounts of labour.

46
00:03:49.921 --> 00:03:54.552
That shows Denmark was one kingdom, gathered under one central power.

47
00:03:55.102 --> 00:03:57.627
Now let's follow the Vikings abroad.

48
00:03:57.577 --> 00:04:03.439
They had a lot of contact with the outside world, and the development of the Viking ships made that possible.

49
00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:07.802
These ships were both more solid and more flexible than earlier ships.

50
00:04:07.802 --> 00:04:15.764
They could sail the open sea even in difficult conditions, travel quickly, and even sail up rivers to towns that were not on the coast.

51
00:04:15.764 --> 00:04:20.970
The Vikings traded with the rest of Europe, and they also had contact with the Arab countries.

52
00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.070
But not everything was peaceful trade.

53
00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:32.982
In the 800s, Danish Vikings also went on raids, to England and France, and as far away as Constantinople, today's Istanbul.

54
00:04:32.982 --> 00:04:38.245
On these raids they plundered monasteries and towns and demanded tax from the locals.

55
00:04:38.245 --> 00:04:43.807
They also conquered large areas of land, and some Vikings settled in the places they conquered.

56
00:04:43.807 --> 00:04:46.308
One example is Normandy in France.

57
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.700
Then comes the high point.

58
00:04:48.650 --> 00:05:00.562
The Danish king Svend Tveskæg, who reigned from nine eighty-seven to ten fourteen, conquered England, and became its king in the year 1013, that is, in the 1000s.

59
00:05:00.562 --> 00:05:05.413
His son, Knud den Store, reigned from ten eighteen to ten thirty-five.

60
00:05:05.413 --> 00:05:12.538
He was also king of England, and he ruled Norway as well, at the moment when the Viking empire was at its largest.

61
00:05:12.538 --> 00:05:15.504
But that great kingdom fell apart again quickly.

62
00:05:16.054 --> 00:05:19.104
Yet the Vikings left lasting marks in England.

63
00:05:19.054 --> 00:05:26.441
The English people took in many words from the Vikings' language, Old Norse, which is the forerunner of modern Danish.

64
00:05:26.441 --> 00:05:31.691
Modern English is estimated to contain a couple of hundred words from Old Norse.

65
00:05:31.691 --> 00:05:36.282
Examples: egg, window, knife, law, and sell.

66
00:05:36.832 --> 00:05:39.257
Now, what did the Vikings believe?

67
00:05:39.207 --> 00:05:47.407
In the Danish area, people practised a Nordic religion called Asatru, which means belief in the Aesir, who were gods.

68
00:05:47.407 --> 00:05:53.757
Central to it were sacrifices, rituals and ceremonies in which gifts were given to the gods.

69
00:05:53.757 --> 00:06:00.257
These could be jewellery, food, tools, but also animals and people, who were killed for the occasion.

70
00:06:00.257 --> 00:06:08.820
This could take place in nature, at special holy places such as forests, springs or bogs, or by the houses where the Vikings lived.

71
00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.420
The king of the gods was Odin.

72
00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:17.320
He is often described as an older man with only one eye and two ravens sitting on his shoulders.

73
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:26.620
Among the other gods: Freja, goddess of fertility, and Thor, god of thunder and war, who drove across the sky in his chariot.

74
00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.870
According to the myth, that was the sound people heard when it thundered.

75
00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.054
Thor's hammer was called Mjølner.

76
00:06:33.604 --> 00:06:36.554
And these gods live on in the Danish language.

77
00:06:36.504 --> 00:06:41.229
Wednesday, onsdag, comes from Odin, and Thursday, torsdag, from Thor.

78
00:06:41.229 --> 00:06:44.129
Many place names have roots in mythology too.

79
00:06:44.129 --> 00:06:48.366
Odense comes from "Odin's vi", meaning Odin's sanctuary.

80
00:06:48.366 --> 00:06:54.048
And Tórshavn, the largest town in the Faroe Islands, is of course Thor's harbour.

81
00:06:54.604 --> 00:07:02.254
But then something new arrived: Christianity came to Denmark in the Viking Age, and slowly replaced the old religion.

82
00:07:02.204 --> 00:07:08.317
The German missionary Ansgar built churches in the middle of the 800s in Hedeby and Ribe.

83
00:07:08.317 --> 00:07:13.029
And around the year 965, Harald Blåtand was baptised.

84
00:07:13.029 --> 00:07:20.867
The runestone he raised at Jelling says that King Harald won all of Denmark and Norway for himself and made the Danes Christian.

85
00:07:20.867 --> 00:07:25.829
That is why Harald's Jelling stone is often called "Denmark's baptismal certificate".

86
00:07:25.829 --> 00:07:28.224
A good one to remember for the exam.

87
00:07:28.774 --> 00:07:30.799
So that is the Viking Age.

88
00:07:30.749 --> 00:07:35.599
Now we step into the Middle Ages, roughly ten fifty to fifteen hundred.

89
00:07:35.599 --> 00:07:41.211
This was an age in which the king, the church and the nobility often fought each other for power.

90
00:07:41.211 --> 00:07:45.586
Over time, royal power and church grew closer together.

91
00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:53.736
And it was also a period of important innovations, such as the Jutland Law, and of the worst plague in Danish history, the Black Death.

92
00:07:53.736 --> 00:07:59.490
And finally, the whole of the Nordic region was gathered in one union, the Kalmar Union.

93
00:08:00.076 --> 00:08:02.288
Let's start with the struggle for power.

94
00:08:02.238 --> 00:08:07.863
After Knud den Store died in ten thirty-five, an unstable period followed.

95
00:08:07.863 --> 00:08:11.964
A number of Danish kings were murdered, which weakened royal power.

96
00:08:11.964 --> 00:08:18.214
A famous example is Knud the Holy, Knud den Hellige, who reigned from ten eighty to eighty-six.

97
00:08:18.214 --> 00:08:25.563
He was killed in Saint Alban's Church in Odense in the year 1086 by the magnates, the richest men in the country.

98
00:08:25.563 --> 00:08:31.440
They were unhappy that they had to pay fines to the king if they did not take part in military service.

99
00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:39.140
And that murder made one thing clear: in the future, kings could only rule if they made agreements with the magnates.

100
00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:45.802
These agreements developed into real contracts about how power should be shared between the king and the magnates.

101
00:08:45.802 --> 00:08:47.802
They were called håndfæstninger.

102
00:08:47.802 --> 00:08:54.965
The first håndfæstning was signed in 1282 by Erik Klipping, who reigned from twelve fifty-nine to eighty-six.

103
00:08:54.965 --> 00:08:58.890
The last was signed by Frederik the Third in 1648.

104
00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:04.915
It was then cancelled when the absolute hereditary monarchy was introduced in sixteen sixty to sixty-one.

105
00:09:04.915 --> 00:09:10.122
Remember that: first in 1282, last in 1648.

106
00:09:10.696 --> 00:09:12.996
Meanwhile the church was changing too.

107
00:09:12.946 --> 00:09:25.859
For the first hundred years or more after Harald Blåtand was baptised, Christianity in Denmark was subject to the Archbishop of Hamburg-Bremen in Germany, who was strongly influenced by the German emperors.

108
00:09:25.859 --> 00:09:29.984
That weakened the Danish king, and so Denmark's independence.

109
00:09:29.984 --> 00:09:33.734
But during the 1000s an independent Danish church emerged.

110
00:09:33.734 --> 00:09:41.580
It was divided into eight dioceses, that is, geographical areas administered by the church with a bishop as the top leader.

111
00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:53.117
Then, in the year 1103, Lund in Skåne, which was part of Denmark until 1658, got its own archbishop, with the whole Nordic region under him.

112
00:09:53.067 --> 00:10:03.630
The North thereby became an independent church area, as part of the Catholic church, directly under the Pope in Rome, and independent of the German emperor's power.

113
00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:10.267
A few hundred years after Christianity was introduced, the church had become very powerful.

114
00:10:10.218 --> 00:10:13.817
It was an important part of every person's everyday life.

115
00:10:13.817 --> 00:10:20.767
People had to pay a tax to the church called tithe, "tiende", because it was one tenth of the harvest or other production.

116
00:10:20.767 --> 00:10:29.160
The church largely decided what people were allowed to believe, how to live, and which actions were right and which were wrong, or sinful.

117
00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:38.098
The centre of faith was the town's or village's church, and around 1250, up to three thousand churches had been built in Denmark.

118
00:10:38.048 --> 00:10:45.935
People went there to pray to God, and to receive forgiveness from the priest if they had, in the church's eyes, done something wrong.

119
00:10:45.935 --> 00:10:52.598
Forgiveness of sin could often be bought by paying money to the church, which was called indulgence, "aflad".

120
00:10:52.598 --> 00:10:58.938
And the church service, the mass, every Sunday created reconciliation between God and people.

121
00:10:59.488 --> 00:11:05.038
Monasteries were also built, where monks and nuns could live the perfect Christian life.

122
00:11:04.988 --> 00:11:08.301
Many monasteries also took care of the sick and the poor.

123
00:11:08.301 --> 00:11:17.313
The church soon came to own large areas of land in Denmark, and the Catholic church was an international organisation with great influence in Europe.

124
00:11:17.313 --> 00:11:21.420
So the medieval Danish kings had to make sure the church was on their side.

125
00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:28.583
From the middle of the 1100s to the middle of the 1200s, royal power regained strength.

126
00:11:28.533 --> 00:11:36.958
And that can be seen in a number of laws made at this time, of which the best known is the Jutland Law, Jyske Lov, from the year 1241.

127
00:11:36.958 --> 00:11:39.233
Now it was the king who made the laws.

128
00:11:39.233 --> 00:11:43.358
This was important for the Danish legal society in the Middle Ages.

129
00:11:43.358 --> 00:11:48.120
Before, families had taken revenge on each other when crimes were committed.

130
00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.420
The Jutland Law rejected this kind of blood revenge.

131
00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:57.318
It also abolished large parts of customary law, which was based on unwritten rules.

132
00:11:57.868 --> 00:12:06.568
The sentence "With law shall the land be built", "Med lov skal man land bygge", comes from the introduction to the Jutland Law.

133
00:12:06.518 --> 00:12:17.112
At first the law applied only in Jutland, but large parts of it were later written into the Danish Law of 1683, the first common law for the whole of Denmark.

134
00:12:17.662 --> 00:12:22.125
The 1200s also saw Danish kings carry out a number of crusades.

135
00:12:22.075 --> 00:12:30.200
These were military actions in the Baltic area to convert pagan, that is non-Christian, populations to Christianity.

136
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.662
One of the best known went to Estonia in 1219.

137
00:12:33.662 --> 00:12:41.154
The story goes that during a battle near Tallinn, the Dannebrog fell down from the sky and made sure the Danes won the battle.

138
00:12:41.704 --> 00:12:43.979
Now to the plague, and the union.

139
00:12:43.929 --> 00:12:53.042
From the middle of the 1200s, Denmark was marked by division and civil war, which increased in the first half of the 1300s.

140
00:12:53.042 --> 00:13:02.317
King Valdemar Atterdag, who reigned from thirteen forty to seventy-five, managed to gather the Danish kingdom again and rebuild state power.

141
00:13:02.317 --> 00:13:06.017
During his reign, the plague called the Black Death raged.

142
00:13:06.017 --> 00:13:12.379
It hit Denmark and the rest of Europe around 1350, and killed up to half of the population.

143
00:13:12.379 --> 00:13:14.717
That is the 1300s.

144
00:13:14.717 --> 00:13:22.044
Yet the tragedy strengthened the king's power, because he took over much of the land that had belonged to those who died of the plague.

145
00:13:22.594 --> 00:13:29.282
After Valdemar Atterdag died in 1375, his daughter Margrete the First took power.

146
00:13:29.232 --> 00:13:36.519
She also became regent in Norway, which at that time included the Faroe Islands, Iceland and parts of Greenland.

147
00:13:36.519 --> 00:13:47.082
And in 1397 she gathered Denmark, Norway and Sweden, which at that time also included Finland, in a union with Denmark as the dominant power.

148
00:13:47.082 --> 00:13:53.032
This was called the Kalmar Union, because the union treaty was signed in the Swedish town of Kalmar.

149
00:13:53.032 --> 00:13:59.032
The Kalmar Union was finally dissolved in 1523, when Sweden broke free for good.

150
00:13:59.032 --> 00:14:02.982
Norway stayed in union with Denmark until 1814.

151
00:14:02.982 --> 00:14:06.078
Margrete is buried in Roskilde Cathedral.

152
00:14:06.628 --> 00:14:09.228
Now it is time for your exam practice.

153
00:14:09.178 --> 00:14:14.091
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

154
00:14:14.091 --> 00:14:21.576
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

155
00:14:22.126 --> 00:14:23.376
Question 1.

156
00:14:23.326 --> 00:14:27.639
Which three countries formed the Kalmar Union in 1397?

157
00:14:27.639 --> 00:14:30.976
Option A: Denmark, Sweden and England.

158
00:14:30.976 --> 00:14:34.401
Option B: Denmark, Norway and Sweden.

159
00:14:34.401 --> 00:14:37.770
Option C: Denmark, England and Norway.
[DA] Hvilke tre lande dannede Kalmarunionen i 1397?  A: Danmark, Sverige og England  |  B: Danmark, Norge og Sverige  |  C: Danmark, England og Norge

160
00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:48.070
The answer is B: Denmark, Norway and Sweden.

161
00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.700
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Danmark, Norge og Sverige

162
00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:52.530
Question 2.

163
00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:56.818
Which king had it written on one of the Jelling stones that he made the Danes Christian?

164
00:14:56.818 --> 00:14:59.255
Option A: Svend Tveskæg.

165
00:14:59.255 --> 00:15:01.605
Option B: Harald Blåtand.

166
00:15:01.605 --> 00:15:04.812
Option C: Valdemar the Victorious.
[DA] Hvilken konge fik skrevet på den ene af Jellingstenene, at han gjorde danskerne (danerne) kristne?  A: Svend Tveskæg  |  B: Harald Blåtand  |  C: Valdemar Sejr

167
00:15:11.392 --> 00:15:13.967
The answer is B: Harald Blåtand.

168
00:15:13.917 --> 00:15:16.644
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Harald Blåtand

169
00:15:17.224 --> 00:15:18.562
Question 3.

170
00:15:18.512 --> 00:15:23.799
When did King Harald Blåtand have it written on one of the Jelling stones that he made the Danes Christian?

171
00:15:23.799 --> 00:15:26.449
Option A: The 900s.

172
00:15:26.449 --> 00:15:29.024
Option B: The 1200s.

173
00:15:29.024 --> 00:15:31.932
Option C: The 1500s.
[DA] Hvornår fik kong Harald Blåtand skrevet på den ene af Jellingstenene, at han gjorde danerne (danskerne) kristne?  A: 900-tallet  |  B: 1200-tallet  |  C: 1500-tallet

174
00:15:38.482 --> 00:15:41.282
The answer is A: The 900s.

175
00:15:41.232 --> 00:15:43.902
It appeared in the May 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: 900-tallet

176
00:15:44.452 --> 00:15:45.752
Question 4.

177
00:15:45.702 --> 00:15:48.677
Which are names of gods in the Viking religion Asatro?

178
00:15:48.677 --> 00:15:51.140
Option A: Thyra and Gorm.

179
00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.952
Option B: Ingrid and Absalon.

180
00:15:53.952 --> 00:15:56.568
Option C: Freja and Thor.
[DA] Hvilke af følgende er navne på guder i vikingetidens religion 'asatroen'?  A: Thyra og Gorm  |  B: Ingrid og Absalon  |  C: Freja og Thor

181
00:16:03.118 --> 00:16:06.006
The answer is C: Freja and Thor.

182
00:16:05.956 --> 00:16:10.146
It appeared in the May 2024 and June 2026 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Freja og Thor

183
00:16:10.696 --> 00:16:12.159
Question 5.

184
00:16:12.109 --> 00:16:18.946
Which religion was the most widespread in Denmark in the Middle Ages, roughly 1050 to 1500?

185
00:16:18.946 --> 00:16:22.009
Option A: Orthodox Christianity.

186
00:16:22.009 --> 00:16:24.771
Option B: Catholic Christianity.

187
00:16:24.771 --> 00:16:27.732
Option C: Protestant Christianity.
[DA] Hvilken religion var den mest udbredte i Danmark i middelalderen (cirka 1050-1500)?  A: Ortodoks kristendom  |  B: Katolsk kristendom  |  C: Protestantisk kristendom

188
00:16:34.282 --> 00:16:37.295
The answer is B: Catholic Christianity.

189
00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:41.790
It appeared in the June 2022 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Katolsk kristendom

190
00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:43.778
Question 6.

191
00:16:43.728 --> 00:16:48.428
What characterised Denmark in the Middle Ages, 1050 to 1500?

192
00:16:48.428 --> 00:16:52.840
Option A: Denmark got colonies in Africa and the Caribbean.

193
00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:55.978
Option B: Thousands of churches were built.

194
00:16:55.978 --> 00:16:59.832
Option C: Denmark got a democratic constitution.
[DA] Hvad kendetegnede Danmark i middelalderen (1050-1500)?  A: Danmark fik kolonier i Afrika og Caribien  |  B: Der blev bygget tusindvis af kirker  |  C: Danmark fik en demokratisk grundlov

195
00:17:06.382 --> 00:17:09.757
The answer is B: Thousands of churches were built.

196
